  "summary": "Bauta, moretta and medico della peste — Venice's licensed anonymity in leather, gilding and beaked masks.",
  "description": "Venice made anonymity a civic art: from the 1200s onward carnival seasons licensed citizens to vanish behind white volto masks, the black bauta with its jutting chin and tricorno hat, the button-held moretta silencing noblewomen into mystery, and the beaked white medico della peste that plague doctors wore during 1600s outbreaks. Maskmakers (mascareri) held guild status from 1436, gilding and tooling leather, and their disguises structured gambling seasons, assignations and street justice. Banned with the Republic's fall in 1797, the craft revived in 1979 and masks now flood the winter carnival.",
